<BaseLayout
  title="Publication, licensing & intellectual property · Stavros Kousidis"
  description="Publication expectations for thesis software, open-source licensing, commercial use, and arrangements for industry collaborations."
>
  <article class="note-page">
    <header class="note-header">
      <p class="kicker">Thesis supervision · Publication policy</p>
      <h1>Publication, licensing &amp; intellectual property.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Open source is the usual starting point for thesis-developed software.
        Commercial use is welcome; confidentiality and proprietary requirements
        need to be resolved before a thesis begins.
      </p>
      <p class="publication-back">
        <a class="text-link" href="/theses/#open-source-and-ip">← Back to supervision expectations</a>
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="note-body">
      <h2>Why open source?</h2>
      <p>
        Reusable software makes technical results easier to inspect, reproduce, improve,
        and build upon. For theses that develop software, I normally expect newly
        developed code, tests, and reproducibility tools to be released publicly
        by project completion. You can use a private repository while developing.
        This is a supervision expectation, not a claim that every university thesis
        is subject to one universal license.
      </p>

      <h2>Can open-source software be used commercially?</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Yes. Open source does not mean non-commercial.</strong> Open-source
        software may be sold, used in commercial products, and offered as part of
        paid services. The important distinction is between <em>commercial use</em>
        and <em>proprietary redistribution</em>. Some licenses permit proprietary
        derivatives, while copyleft licenses impose conditions when modified or
        combined software is distributed. The obligations depend on the particular
        license and how software components interact.
      </p>

      <h2>Which license should I use?</h2>
      <p>
        The <a href="https://opensource.org/">Open Source Initiative (OSI)</a>
        reviews licenses against the
        <a href="https://opensource.org/osd">Open Source Definition</a>. We agree
        on an appropriate <a href="https://opensource.org/licenses">OSI-approved license</a>
        before thesis registration. It must be compatible with the licenses of
        dependencies and any existing project to which you contribute.
        For example, a KDE-related component may need a KDE-compatible license.
        A license's exact version, compatibility, and obligations matter more than
        a generic label such as “GPL”.
      </p>

      <h2>What changes in an industry collaboration?</h2>
      <p>
        A company may have legitimate confidentiality, intellectual-property,
        or proprietary-development requirements. An exception to the usual
        publication expectation may be possible, but it must be explicitly
        agreed with the student, supervisor, and relevant institutional or
        industry representatives <strong>before registration</strong>. The
        arrangement must comply with applicable university requirements and
        preserve the ability to examine and independently assess the thesis.
        A topic with an explicit mandatory open-source condition may not
        accommodate such an exception.
      </p>

      <h2>What if an existing open-source license conflicts?</h2>
      <p>
        No agreement with a partner can remove obligations owed under an existing
        third-party license. In particular, distributing a derivative of copyleft
        software under incompatible proprietary terms may not be permitted.
        Possible solutions include choosing a compatible architecture or license,
        obtaining additional permission from rights holders, or changing the
        research scope. If no lawful and academically viable arrangement is
        possible, <strong>that thesis topic cannot proceed in the proposed form</strong>.
      </p>

      <h2>What must remain private?</h2>
      <p>
        Open-source code publication does <strong>not</strong> require publishing
        the thesis manuscript, third-party datasets, confidential business
        information, private correspondence, credentials, cryptographic keys,
        or sensitive vulnerability details. Use synthetic or authorized data in
        reproducible examples. Rights to publish code or other results must be
        clarified with relevant contributors and rights holders.
      </p>

      <h2>Before you register</h2>
      <p>
        We determine the intended software deliverables, upstream licenses,
        publication scope and timing, rights to release the work, and any
        justified confidentiality restrictions. If a specific
        <a href="/theses/">thesis topic</a> states stricter publication
        requirements, those take precedence over this general expectation.
      </p>

      <p>
        For general orientation, see the
        <a href="https://opensource.org/faq">OSI FAQ</a> and, for KDE-related
        contributions, the <a href="https://community.kde.org/Policies/Licensing_Policy">KDE licensing policy</a>.
        The specific arrangements for a thesis should be agreed with the relevant parties;
        this page does not replace legal or institutional review.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
